 ## Overview
-Pixel separation for bright foreground is a technique used in silhouette image space acceleration to correct the incorrect blurring of bright pixels located at low depths (foreground) against dark backgrounds [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem where standard luminance-weighted blurring causes bright foreground objects to lose intensity or blend incorrectly into the background by splitting the input scattering image into two separate components: one for separated bright pixels ($L_{ssc}$) and one for the rest ($L_{sc}$) [S1, unofficial]. This method sets itself apart from its sibling, the Visual Hull Oracle Kernel, by explicitly isolating high-luminance, low-depth pixels to prevent their energy from being suppressed during the MIP chain construction, whereas the sibling focuses on visual hull approximation [S1, unofficial].
+Pixel separation for bright foreground is a technique used in silhouette image acquisition and filtering to correct the incorrect blurring of bright pixels located at low depths (foreground) against dark backgrounds [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem where standard luminance-weighted blurring causes bright foreground objects to lose intensity or blend incorrectly by splitting the input scattering image into two separate components: one for separated bright pixels ($L_{\text{ssc}}$) and one for the rest ($L_{\text{sc}}$) [S1, unofficial].
 
